          "literatureReview": "Widal test detects agglutinating antibodies to Salmonella antigens. Normal: <1:80 titer. Significant: ≥1:160 for O antigen, ≥1:320 for H antigen. Tests for S. typhi (O, H) and S. paratyphi (A, B). Becomes positive 7-10 days after infection. False positives with other salmonella infections or cross-reacting antibodies. False negatives in early infection. Single high titer more significant than rising titer. Confirm with blood culture. Used for typhoid fever diagnosis in endemic areas. Critical for enteric fever diagnosis and outbreak investigation."
